  "answer_text": "The bump in the throat commonly known as the **Adam\u2019s apple** is anatomically called the **laryngeal prominence**. It is a visible protrusion formed by the **thyroid cartilage**, a tough but flexible shield of tissue that wraps around and protects the larynx (voice box) and vocal cords. \n\nWhile everyone (both male and female) has thyroid cartilage, men have much larger and more prominent Adam\u2019s apples due to two main biological factors: **testosterone** and **anatomy geometry**.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Role of Testosterone During Puberty\nBefore puberty, boys and girls have similarly sized voice boxes. However, when puberty begins:\n* **In biological males:** A massive surge of **testosterone** stimulates the larynx and its surrounding thyroid cartilage to grow rapidly.\n* **The result:** The vocal cords inside the larynx grow longer and thicker (similar to the thick strings on a bass guitar), which is what causes the male voice to deepen. To accommodate this much larger voice box, the protective thyroid cartilage must grow significantly larger as well.\n\n### 2. The Angle of the Cartilage\nThe thyroid cartilage is made of two flat plates (laminae) that meet and fuse together at the front of the neck. The sharpness of the angle where these plates meet dictates how much the bump sticks out:\n* **In males:** Because the larynx grows so large, these plates fuse at a sharp, acute angle of about **90 degrees** (almost a right angle). This sharp angle juts forward under the skin like the bow of a ship, creating a very prominent bump.\n* **In females:** Because the female larynx does not undergo the same testosterone-driven growth, the plates fuse at a wider, more obtuse angle of about **120 degrees**. This wider angle keeps the cartilage flat and relatively flush against the neck, making it mostly invisible.\n\n---\n\n### Do women have an Adam's apple?\n**Yes, but it is rarely visible.** Females have the exact same anatomical structure protecting their vocal cords. However, because of their wider cartilage angle and typically higher levels of subcutaneous fat on the neck, it remains hidden. That being said, some women with naturally larger voice boxes, lower body fat, or higher levels of testosterone may have a slightly visible Adam\u2019s apple.\n\n### Where does the name come from?\nThe colloquial name \"Adam's apple\" comes from a centuries-old biblical folklore. According to the legend, when Adam ate the forbidden fruit in the Garden of Eden, a piece of the apple got stuck in his throat, leaving a permanent bump that was passed down to all men. Physically, however, it is entirely made of cartilage and has nothing to do with food.",
